{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head 25543905262cd93894ca9977d41252aec9acd3de, against the four candidates at that head: 42442c3a at cmd/gs, 1d1de4de at internal/app, b661e0dd at AGENTS.md, 8181c5db at SKILL.md. All four are effective, live and non-stale and name that exact commit. I took no authority from the old head 4a751e5e, report 1bf94679 or the withdrawn review 457f5245.\n\nPLACEMENT AND COVERAGE. The guard is in validateReportBasis in internal/app, called from BuildActRequest — the same shared boundary that carries the report-no-promise check, and pre-append: the test asserts the refused approval leaves head and depth unchanged, so nothing is signed and nothing is submitted. Every write path crosses it, which I checked by enumerating the callers rather than assuming: the MCP adapter at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:879, the GitHub connector at cmd/gitseq-github/main.go:454, the gs CLI at cmd/gs/main.go:1403, and Workspace.Act at internal/app/app.go:708, which is what gs state and gs batch use. There is no surface that reaches the log without passing through it. It fires only for report-lifecycle kinds, because validateReportBasis returns early otherwise.\n\nMULTI-ARTIFACT APPROVALS. Compatible, and I proved it by filing this verdict rather than by reading. The guard scans rests_on for the named artifact and returns on the first match, so extra bases are simply extra; an approval resting on more than it names passes. This verdict rests on four artifacts and names one of them, which is the reviewer-signed-set shape the merge receipt bound depends on, and it was admitted.\n\nMUTATION. Both directions at this head. Replacing the guard body with a discard makes TestApprovedReportMustRestOnItsNamedArtifactBeforeSigning fail with \"an approved report was signed without its named artifact basis\"; restored, it passes, and its second half files a correct approval and asserts the fold rules it effective. The test also pins the message, requiring it to name the artifact and to point at gs review.\n\nDOCS. AGENTS.md and SKILL.md each add one sentence: file the verdict with gs review, and a verdict filed by hand must rest on the artifact it names. That is advice to an author, and it is stricter than the guard rather than looser — the guard refuses only an approved verdict that names a non-empty artifact — so it cannot mislead anyone into filing something that is refused, and it makes no claim about protection that the code does not deliver. This is the safe direction of the trap, and I checked it deliberately.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are internal/app, the application host and admission boundary, and the repository policy documents; cmd/gs changes only a test. architecture.md already describes internal/app as the layer that applies application admission, and this adds one more admission rule inside that contract rather than changing it, so I do not think this head needs an architecture.md edit or a candidate there. The kernel, storage, nexus and projection boundaries are untouched.\n\nSECURITY. The trust boundary is approval authority: a ratified approval authorizes a merge and, since the receipt work, bounds what that merge may retire, so a malformed approval is worth catching early. This is defense in depth and not a replacement — validateMerge still refuses at cmd/gs/main.go:885 with \"approval does not rest on its named artifact\", so a record signed by an older binary, by another implementation, or submitted raw is still caught at the gate that moves main. Both checks exist at this head and I verified each independently. Two gaps I judge acceptable and record so they are not rediscovered as surprises: a changes-requested verdict naming an artifact it does not rest on is not refused, which is harmless because only approvals are consumed as authority; and an approved report with an empty artifact field is not refused here, which validateMerge catches. Neither weakens the merge gate.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. The guard is nine lines in the function that already does this job, reuses the body it was already given, and adds no new surface or flag.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head in a detached worktree: git diff --check clean, go vet ./... clean, go build ./... clean, go test ./... -count=1 fully green with all 25 packages ok and exit 0, and go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1 green on its own at 81.5s. Serialized by a ps check with no other runner active; flock is not installed on this machine. Not merged.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:42442c3a4bb02fab2b93ae28157643b71005702f","head":"25543905262cd93894ca9977d41252aec9acd3de","verdict":"approved"}}